{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EQWkE3tG7pcbYRCjgLWASM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# SKILLER7\n2\t\n3\t*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\n4\t\n5\t**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \"Killer7\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\n6\twith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\n7\tways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\n8\t`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\n9\t\n10\t**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\n11\tfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\n12\tfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\n13\tthem \"guests\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\n14\ton rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\n15\tThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\n16\tbefore they reach you.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## 0. How to use this plan\n21\t\n22\t- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\n23\t  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\n24\t- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\n25\t  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\n26\t  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\n27\t  art, music, levels or look.\n28\t- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\n29\t  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\n30\t  piece of content in this plan.\n31\t- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\n32\t  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 1. At a glance\n37\t\n38\t| | |\n39\t|---|---|\n40\t| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\n41\t| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\n42\t| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\n43\t| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\n44\t| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\n45\t| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\n46\t| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \"Turn-Down Service\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \"Après\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\n47\t| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\n48\t| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\n49\t| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\n50\t| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\n51\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\n52\t| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\n57\t\n58\t| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\n59\t|---|---|\n60\t| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\n61\t| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\"THAW.\") |\n62\t| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\n63\t| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\n64\t| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\n65\t| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\n66\t| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \"Gratuity is customary.\") |\n67\t| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\n68\t| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\n69\t| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\n74\t\n75\t**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\n76\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\n77\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\n78\tnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\n79\t\n80\t**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\n81\t- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n82\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\n83\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\n84\t  (its `<title>` starts with \"Skiller7\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\n85\t  holds a port.\n86\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\n87\t  the dev folder, and run Node and Playwright with `TMPDIR` pointing at a `tmp/` folder inside it (Chromium's\n88\t  profiles go there). Playwright's Chromium 1243 is already installed in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`.\n89\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n90\t- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\n91\t  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\n96\t\n97\t**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\n98\t- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\n99\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\n100\t- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\n101\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\n102\t\n103\t`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\n104\t\n105\t| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\n106\t|---|---|---|---|\n107\t| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\n108\t| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\n109\t| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\n110\t| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\n111\t| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\n112\t| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\n113\t| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\n114\t| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\n115\t\n116\t**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\n117\t```json\n118\t{ \"imports\": {\n119\t  \"three\":        \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n120\t  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n121\t  \"three/tsl\":    \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n122\t  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }\n123\t```\n124\t`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\n125\tnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\n126\tdouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\n127\tand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\n128\t\n129\t**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\n130\thandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\n131\tconcatenates (§5.8).\n132\t\n133\t**Verified while planning** (a throwaway spike, not part of the build): one HTML file holding the 2D engine (classic\n134\tscript), the 3D module (inline module) and this import map, opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 through\n135\tPlaywright 1.63.0, loaded three r182 and Rapier from jsdelivr and booted `E.World3D` with no page errors. Headless\n136\tChromium has no WebGPU, so both `?backend=webgl` and the default ran on WebGL 2. A level with `ground: 'snow'`, a\n137\tHumanoid on a card with `pose: false` that our code `place()`d and `rig.update()`d every step, a `'fixed'` camera set in\n138\t`draw`, `world.glow` (it already reads as a Warm Spot), `world.project` and the `cel` filter all rendered as expected.\n139\tTwo lessons: an actor's position reads back from Rapier in float32 (about 3e-6 off), so the sim keeps its own positions\n140\tand never reads actors; and `'fixed'` cameras don't cut walls, so every shot must sit in open space (§7.2).\n141\t\n142\t**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\n143\tfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\n144\twe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\n145\tlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\n146\tengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\n147\tsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## 5. Architecture\n152\t\n153\t### 5.1 The built page, in order\n154\t\n155\t```html\n156\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n157\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n158\t<script>/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 … pasted unmodified … */</script>       <!-- first script, right after charset -->\n159\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n160\t<title>Skiller7 — Seven Killers, One Mountain</title>\n161\t<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Seven killers share one body and one mountain. Ride the rails, stop, scan for the laughing invisible Whiteouts and shoot the warm spot.\">\n162\t<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#d81e2a\">\n163\t<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,…a red 7 on white…\">\n164\t<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Killer7\">\n165\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n166\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n167\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n168\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n169\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n170\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n171\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest down the Black Run wins; crits cut time\">\n172\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Hold W/✕ ski · S/○ stop · RMB/L2 aim · LMB/R2 shoot · Space/L1 scan\">\n173\t<script type=\"importmap\">{ …§4… }</script>\n174\t<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden} #stage{position:relative;width:100%;height:100%}\n175\t#view{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;pointer-events:none;z-index:0} #screen{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;image-rendering:pixelated;z-index:1;background:transparent}</style>\n176\t</head><body>\n177\t<!-- E.bootSplash('SKILLER7') markup: the splash shows while scripts load -->\n178\t<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n179\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js v0.85.2 (with S7 PATCHes) */</script>\n180\t<script>/* the Skiller7 bundle: src + content, classic script, defines globalThis.S7, starts nothing */</script>\n181\t<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js v0.85.2: imports three and Rapier through the import map */</script>\n182\t<script type=\"module\">S7.start({ World3D: My3D2dge.World3D });</script>\n183\t<script>/* watchdog: if S7.started is still false after 20 s, show \"The 3D libraries didn't load. Check the connection and reload.\" with a Reload button; in a session, call AIHub.fail(that message) */</script>\n184\t</body></html>\n185\t```\n186\tThe build escapes every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n187\t\n188\t### 5.2 Sim and presenter\n189\t\n190\t```\n191\t            game.input ──► Intent layer ◄── Bot (attract, tests)\n192\t                               │ intents (move, turn, branch, aim yaw/pitch deltas, fire, scan, skill, interact…)\n193\t                               ▼\n194\t  ┌──────────────── SIM (deterministic, fixed step 1/120 s, runs in Node) ────────────────┐\n195\t  │ rails · walker · camera director · personas · weapons · skills · whiteouts · combat  │\n196\t  │ scan · bosses · economy · items · gates · story/beats · saves · results · echo run    │\n197\t  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘\n198\t                     state (read only)                   events (S7.bus)\n199\t                                 ▼                              ▼\n200\t        Presenter3D (browser) / NullPresenter (Node)      Audio · UI widgets · HUD\n201\t        World3D worlds, actors, rigs, pen, glows,         (headless-safe; audio no-ops\n202\t        lights, FX, first-person overlay, camera          without an AudioContext)\n203\t```\n204\t- **The sim** owns all game state. It uses only the 2D engine (`E.Path`, `E.rng`, `E.Story`, `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`,\n205\t  `game.input`, `game.audio.pos`, `game.saves`, `game.script`, `game.tween`) and our own math. It never touches three.js,\n206\t  the DOM or `E.World3D`, and it never reads anything back from the presenter.\n207\t- **The presenter** turns state into pictures. It has two hooks: `step(dt)` in the play scene's update after\n208\t  `sim.update` (mirror actors, `rig.update`, `world.step`) and `draw(r)` (pen, glows, lights, `world.draw(r)`, then the\n209\t  HUD). **NullPresenter** has the same interface and does nothing; Node tests use it.\n210\t- **Mouse picking goes through intents.** The UI projects world anchors with `world.project` and turns a hover or a\n211\t  click into an intent (`branch = 2`, `interact = 'murmel'`). The sim also keeps its own simple projection (from the\n212\t  camera director's pose) for stick-to-branch mapping, so headless play needs no presenter.\n213\t- Level geometry exists twice from one source: the presenter builds `World3D` from the area's rows or terrain\n214\t  function, and the sim builds a `LevelGrid` from the same data for line of sight and paths.\n215\t\n216\t### 5.3 Source tree (dev folder)\n217\t\n218\t```\n219\tSkiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/\n220\t  package.json            { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" },\n221\t                            scripts: { build: \"node tools/build.mjs\", test: \"node tests/run.mjs\" } }\n222\t  engine/                 a copy of dist/handoff/ (my-3d2dge.js patched; kits/, tools/, src/assets/…) + my-3d2dge-3d.js (§4)\n223\t  vendor/                 three-0.182.0/, rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/, aihub/               (§4)\n224\t  ref/                    3D starter slices, for reading\n225\t  src/\n226\t    00-core.js        S7 namespace, VERSION, S7.bus, registries (S7.area/persona/weapon/enemy/item/beat/song/voice),\n227\t                      named E.rng streams ('spawn','spread','laugh','fx'), units (M = 16 world units = 1 m)\n228\t    01-math.js        vectors, ray–sphere, ray–capsule, segment distance, angles, easing helpers\n229\t    02-tuning.js      TUNING table {key: {def, min, max, step, label, group}}, live values, reset, difficulty multipliers\n230\t    05-input.js       the S7 input preset, intent layer (human and bot), last-device tracking, idle timer,\n231\t                      touch-only detection, mouse modes (look / pointer), stick flick detection\n232\t    10-levelgrid.js   LevelGrid from level rows and legend (floor and wall heights, pits, stairs, platforms),\n233\t                      terrain sampler, line of sight (tile DDA with heights / terrain ray-march), BFS flow fields\n234\t    11-rails.js       RailGraph (nodes, edges as E.Path), Walker (walk, ski, lift, jump, stairs), junctions,\n235\t                      gate checks, spot proximity\n236\t    12-camera.js      CameraDirector: shots, cuts, blends, aim transition, overrides, shake; sim-side projection\n237\t    20-personas.js    roster state, HP, stats, availability, switching, frozen/fallen\n238\t    21-weapons.js     fire cycles, magazines, reloads, spread, recoil, hitscan/cone/lob kinds, effective range\n239\t    22-skills.js      flare, tuck, longlook, makeitrain + buyout, echo, flash, charge, rescue\n240\t    30-whiteouts.js   enemy types, behaviours, warm spots, tells (footprints, breath), laugh scheduling,\n241\t                      spawner, waves\n242\t    31-combat.js      damage pipeline; hits on enemies, props and targets; crits; explosions; projectiles\n243\t                      (snowballs, suitcases, medals, shells); drops and pickup magnet\n244\t    32-scan.js        scan pulse, reveal timers, laugh-direction cues\n245\t    33-bosses.js      boss controllers: grau, jollyfour, hansi (with phases)\n246\t    40-economy.js     Red, Rime, healing, Wax Bench costs and effects\n247\t    41-items.js       inventory, key items, collectibles, Dossier entries\n248\t    42-gates.js       gate kinds: rope, jump, farbell, buyout, echowall, crystal, exposure, codelock, cornice,\n249\t                      serac, icelock, edelweiss, key\n250\t    50-story.js       E.Story setup, people (speaker ↔ rig ↔ voice), flags, objectives, beats (game.script),\n251\t                      title cards, letterbox\n252\t    51-npcs.js        Murmel hints, Pim appearances, phones, frozen tableaux\n253\t    52-room7.js       entering and leaving, the clock, the Wax Bench, the Wardrobe, the bed, the telephone\n254\t    60-save.js        serialize/deserialize, game.saves wiring, autosave, versions and migrate\n255\t    61-results.js     per-target stats and ranks\n256\t    70-audio.js       buses, song table, SFX definitions, positional helper, music state (layers, cues), ambience\n257\t    71-voice.js       Animalese voice synth and its dialog hookup\n258\t    72-laugh.js       laughter synth (styles) and positional emitters\n259\t    80-ui-kit.js      canvas widget kit: focus lists, buttons, sliders, toggles, tabs, grids, glyph prompts;\n260\t                      pad, keys and mouse (hover focuses, click activates, drag moves sliders)\n261\t    81-hud.js         the in-play HUD\n262\t    82-screens.js     title, Featherbed ring, junction markers, Wax Bench, Wardrobe, map, Dossier and items,\n263\t                      code lock, target cards, results, game over, About/Credits, touch-only notice,\n264\t                      attract overlay\n265\t    83-shell.js       a themed E.Shell (pause, settings, controls, help, saves) and its pause items\n266\t    84-dev.js         dev mode panel and dev overlays\n267\t    85-bot.js         the bot: route runner, rail navigation, combat (used by attract mode and tests)\n268\t    86-attract.js     attract director: vignettes, cheats, any-input exit\n269\t    87-hub.js         AI Hub session flow, Echo Run networking, solo echo recording\n270\t    88-echorun.js     Echo Run rules: course, timing, penalties, results\n271\t    90-present3d.js   Presenter3D: a world per area, actor mirroring, rigs, accessories, pen drawing,\n272\t                      glows, lights, camera\n273\t    91-fx3d.js        snowfall, footprint and red-snow decals, breath, scan ring, splashes, flurry transition\n274\t    92-firstperson.js weapon models and their animations on the overlay pen\n275\t    93-present-null.js NullPresenter\n276\t    95-scenes.js      scenes: title, attract, play (Room 7 is a mode of play), results, credits, gameover,\n277\t                      echorun, hub\n278\t    99-start.js       S7.start(opts): new E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n279\t  content/\n280\t    tuning.js personas.js weapons.js enemies.js items.js shapes.js things3d.js songs.js voices.js\n281\t    attract.js echorun.js\n282\t    areas/  room7.js 00A-lobby.js 00B-thirdfloor.js 01A-village.js 01B-palais.js 01C-blackrun.js\n283\t            01D-gondola.js 01E-archive.js 01F-summit.js\n284\t    story/  common.js t00.js t01.js epilogue.js barks.js\n285\t  tools/   build.mjs  validate-content.mjs  fetch-deps.mjs\n286\t  tests/   run.mjs  lib/node-harness.mjs  lib/browser-harness.mjs  node/*.test.mjs  browser/*.test.mjs\n287\t           routes/golden.js (the playthrough's route)  out/ (screenshots, logs, the hub copy; never shipped)\n288\t```\n289\t`content/things3d.js` only defines `S7.things3d = W3 => { W3.THINGS.chandelier = (pen, t, time) => …; … }`, and\n290\tPresenter3D calls it in its init, so nothing there runs in Node. Everything else in `content/` is plain data and runs\n291\tanywhere.\n292\t\n293\t### 5.4 The play loop and the mode machine\n294\t\n295\tThe play scene (`pausable: true`, `touch: []`):\n296\t```\n297\tupdate(dt):  if (ui.modal) { ui.modal.update(dt); return; }   // dev panel, Wax Bench, map… freeze the sim\n298\t             intents = Intent.read(game.input | bot)            // one place turns devices into intents\n299\t             sim.update(dt, intents)                            // everything in §7, in a fixed order\n300\t             presenter.step(dt)                                 // mirror, rigs, world.step (browser only)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa845e-3b4d-4028-b42c-2fc9910c5b5f","uuid":"480d76f7-8773-4a7f-930f-a6c49e561d7c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T08:31:51.430Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-013140__x696bb3/work/PLAN.md","content":"# SKILLER7\n\n*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\n\n**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \"Killer7\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\nwith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\nways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\n`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\n\n**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\nfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\nfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\nthem \"guests\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\non rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\nThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\nbefore they reach you.\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\n  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\n- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\n  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\n  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\n  art, music, levels or look.\n- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\n  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\n  piece of content in this plan.\n- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\n  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\n\n---\n\n## 1. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\n| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\n| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\n| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\n| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\n| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\n| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \"Turn-Down Service\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \"Après\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\n| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\n| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\n| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\n| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\n\n| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\n|---|---|\n| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\n| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\"THAW.\") |\n| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\n| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\n| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\n| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\n| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \"Gratuity is customary.\") |\n| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\n| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\n| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\n\n**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\nnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\n\n**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\n- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\n  (its `<title>` starts with \"Skiller7\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\n  holds a port.\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\n  the dev folder, and run Node and Playwright with `TMPDIR` pointing at a `tmp/` folder inside it (Chromium's\n  profiles go there). Playwright's Chromium 1243 is already installed in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`.\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\n  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\n\n**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\n- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\n- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\n\n`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\n\n| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\n| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\n| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\n| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\n| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\n| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\n| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\n| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\n\n**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\n```json\n{ \"imports\": {\n  \"three\":        \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/tsl\":    \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }\n```\n`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\nnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\ndouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\nand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\n\n**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\nhandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\nconcatenates (§5.8).\n\n**Verified while planning** (a throwaway spike, not part of the build): one HTML file holding the 2D engine (classic\nscript), the 3D module (inline module) and this import map, opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 through\nPlaywright 1.63.0, loaded three r182 and Rapier from jsdelivr and booted `E.World3D` with no page errors. Headless\nChromium has no WebGPU, so both `?backend=webgl` and the default ran on WebGL 2. A level with `ground: 'snow'`, a\nHumanoid on a card with `pose: false` that our code `place()`d and `rig.update()`d every step, a `'fixed'` camera set in\n`draw`, `world.glow` (it already reads as a Warm Spot), `world.project` and the `cel` filter all rendered as expected.\nTwo lessons: an actor's position reads back from Rapier in float32 (about 3e-6 off), so the sim keeps its own positions\nand never reads actors; and `'fixed'` cameras don't cut walls, so every shot must sit in open space (§7.2).\n\n**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\nfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\nwe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\nlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\nengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\nsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 The built page, in order\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 … pasted unmodified … */</script>       <!-- first script, right after charset -->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Skiller7 — Seven Killers, One Mountain</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Seven killers share one body and one mountain. Ride the rails, stop, scan for the laughing invisible Whiteouts and shoot the warm spot.\">\n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#d81e2a\">\n<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,…a red 7 on white…\">\n<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Killer7\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest down the Black Run wins; crits cut time\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Hold W/✕ ski · S/○ stop · RMB/L2 aim · LMB/R2 shoot · Space/L1 scan\">\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ …§4… }</script>\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden} #stage{position:relative;width:100%;height:100%}\n#view{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;pointer-events:none;z-index:0} #screen{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;image-rendering:pixelated;z-index:1;background:transparent}</style>\n</head><body>\n<!-- E.bootSplash('SKILLER7') markup: the splash shows while scripts load -->\n<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js v0.85.2 (with S7 PATCHes) */</script>\n<script>/* the Skiller7 bundle: src + content, classic script, defines globalThis.S7, starts nothing */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js v0.85.2: imports three and Rapier through the import map */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">S7.start({ World3D: My3D2dge.World3D });</script>\n<script>/* watchdog: if S7.started is still false after 20 s, show \"The 3D libraries didn't load. Check the connection and reload.\" with a Reload button; in a session, call AIHub.fail(that message) */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\nThe build escapes every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n\n### 5.2 Sim and presenter\n\n```\n            game.input ──► Intent layer ◄── Bot (attract, tests)\n                               │ intents (move, turn, branch, aim yaw/pitch deltas, fire, scan, skill, interact…)\n                               ▼\n  ┌──────────────── SIM (deterministic, fixed step 1/120 s, runs in Node) ────────────────┐\n  │ rails · walker · camera director · personas · weapons · skills · whiteouts · combat  │\n  │ scan · bosses · economy · items · gates · story/beats · saves · results · echo run    │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘\n                     state (read only)                   events (S7.bus)\n                                 ▼                              ▼\n        Presenter3D (browser) / NullPresenter (Node)      Audio · UI widgets · HUD\n        World3D worlds, actors, rigs, pen, glows,         (headless-safe; audio no-ops\n        lights, FX, first-person overlay, camera          without an AudioContext)\n```\n- **The sim** owns all game state. It uses only the 2D engine (`E.Path`, `E.rng`, `E.Story`, `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`,\n  `game.input`, `game.audio.pos`, `game.saves`, `game.script`, `game.tween`) and our own math. It never touches three.js,\n  the DOM or `E.World3D`, and it never reads anything back from the presenter.\n- **The presenter** turns state into pictures. It has two hooks: `step(dt)` in the play scene's update after\n  `sim.update` (mirror actors, `rig.update`, `world.step`) and `draw(r)` (pen, glows, lights, `world.draw(r)`, then the\n  HUD). **NullPresenter** has the same interface and does nothing; Node tests use it.\n- **Mouse picking goes through intents.** The UI projects world anchors with `world.project` and turns a hover or a\n  click into an intent (`branch = 2`, `interact = 'murmel'`). The sim also keeps its own simple projection (from the\n  camera director's pose) for stick-to-branch mapping, so headless play needs no presenter.\n- Level geometry exists twice from one source: the presenter builds `World3D` from the area's rows or terrain\n  function, and the sim builds a `LevelGrid` from the same data for line of sight and paths.\n\n### 5.3 Source tree (dev folder)\n\n```\nSkiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/\n  package.json            { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" },\n                            scripts: { build: \"node tools/build.mjs\", test: \"node tests/run.mjs\" } }\n  engine/                 a copy of dist/handoff/ (my-3d2dge.js patched; kits/, tools/, src/assets/…) + my-3d2dge-3d.js (§4)\n  vendor/                 three-0.182.0/, rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/, aihub/               (§4)\n  ref/                    3D starter slices, for reading\n  src/\n    00-core.js        S7 namespace, VERSION, S7.bus, registries (S7.area/persona/weapon/enemy/item/beat/song/voice),\n                      named E.rng streams ('spawn','spread','laugh','fx'), units (M = 16 world units = 1 m)\n    01-math.js        vectors, ray–sphere, ray–capsule, segment distance, angles, easing helpers\n    02-tuning.js      TUNING table {key: {def, min, max, step, label, group}}, live values, reset, difficulty multipliers\n    05-input.js       the S7 input preset, intent layer (human and bot), last-device tracking, idle timer,\n                      touch-only detection, mouse modes (look / pointer), stick flick detection\n    10-levelgrid.js   LevelGrid from level rows and legend (floor and wall heights, pits, stairs, platforms),\n                      terrain sampler, line of sight (tile DDA with heights / terrain ray-march), BFS flow fields\n    11-rails.js       RailGraph (nodes, edges as E.Path), Walker (walk, ski, lift, jump, stairs), junctions,\n                      gate checks, spot proximity\n    12-camera.js      CameraDirector: shots, cuts, blends, aim transition, overrides, shake; sim-side projection\n    20-personas.js    roster state, HP, stats, availability, switching, frozen/fallen\n    21-weapons.js     fire cycles, magazines, reloads, spread, recoil, hitscan/cone/lob kinds, effective range\n    22-skills.js      flare, tuck, longlook, makeitrain + buyout, echo, flash, charge, rescue\n    30-whiteouts.js   enemy types, behaviours, warm spots, tells (footprints, breath), laugh scheduling,\n                      spawner, waves\n    31-combat.js      damage pipeline; hits on enemies, props and targets; crits; explosions; projectiles\n                      (snowballs, suitcases, medals, shells); drops and pickup magnet\n    32-scan.js        scan pulse, reveal timers, laugh-direction cues\n    33-bosses.js      boss controllers: grau, jollyfour, hansi (with phases)\n    40-economy.js     Red, Rime, healing, Wax Bench costs and effects\n    41-items.js       inventory, key items, collectibles, Dossier entries\n    42-gates.js       gate kinds: rope, jump, farbell, buyout, echowall, crystal, exposure, codelock, cornice,\n                      serac, icelock, edelweiss, key\n    50-story.js       E.Story setup, people (speaker ↔ rig ↔ voice), flags, objectives, beats (game.script),\n                      title cards, letterbox\n    51-npcs.js        Murmel hints, Pim appearances, phones, frozen tableaux\n    52-room7.js       entering and leaving, the clock, the Wax Bench, the Wardrobe, the bed, the telephone\n    60-save.js        serialize/deserialize, game.saves wiring, autosave, versions and migrate\n    61-results.js     per-target stats and ranks\n    70-audio.js       buses, song table, SFX definitions, positional helper, music state (layers, cues), ambience\n    71-voice.js       Animalese voice synth and its dialog hookup\n    72-laugh.js       laughter synth (styles) and positional emitters\n    80-ui-kit.js      canvas widget kit: focus lists, buttons, sliders, toggles, tabs, grids, glyph prompts;\n                      pad, keys and mouse (hover focuses, click activates, drag moves sliders)\n    81-hud.js         the in-play HUD\n    82-screens.js     title, Featherbed ring, junction markers, Wax Bench, Wardrobe, map, Dossier and items,\n                      code lock, target cards, results, game over, About/Credits, touch-only notice,\n                      attract overlay\n    83-shell.js       a themed E.Shell (pause, settings, controls, help, saves) and its pause items\n    84-dev.js         dev mode panel and dev overlays\n    85-bot.js         the bot: route runner, rail navigation, combat (used by attract mode and tests)\n    86-attract.js     attract director: vignettes, cheats, any-input exit\n    87-hub.js         AI Hub session flow, Echo Run networking, solo echo recording\n    88-echorun.js     Echo Run rules: course, timing, penalties, results\n    90-present3d.js   Presenter3D: a world per area, actor mirroring, rigs, accessories, pen drawing,\n                      glows, lights, camera\n    91-fx3d.js        snowfall, footprint and red-snow decals, breath, scan ring, splashes, flurry transition\n    92-firstperson.js weapon models and their animations on the overlay pen\n    93-present-null.js NullPresenter\n    95-scenes.js      scenes: title, attract, play (Room 7 is a mode of play), results, credits, gameover,\n                      echorun, hub\n    99-start.js       S7.start(opts): new E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n  content/\n    tuning.js personas.js weapons.js enemies.js items.js shapes.js things3d.js songs.js voices.js\n    attract.js echorun.js\n    areas/  room7.js 00A-lobby.js 00B-thirdfloor.js 01A-village.js 01B-palais.js 01C-blackrun.js\n            01D-gondola.js 01E-archive.js 01F-summit.js\n    story/  common.js t00.js t01.js epilogue.js barks.js\n  tools/   build.mjs  validate-content.mjs  fetch-deps.mjs\n  tests/   run.mjs  lib/node-harness.mjs  lib/browser-harness.mjs  node/*.test.mjs  browser/*.test.mjs\n           routes/golden.js (the playthrough's route)  out/ (screenshots, logs, the hub copy; never shipped)\n```\n`content/things3d.js` only defines `S7.things3d = W3 => { W3.THINGS.chandelier = (pen, t, time) => …; … }`, and\nPresenter3D calls it in its init, so nothing there runs in Node. Everything else in `content/` is plain data and runs\nanywhere.\n\n### 5.4 The play loop and the mode machine\n\nThe play scene (`pausable: true`, `touch: []`):\n```\nupdate(dt):  if (ui.modal) { ui.modal.update(dt); return; }   // dev panel, Wax Bench, map… freeze the sim\n             intents = Intent.read(game.input | bot)            // one place turns devices into intents\n             sim.update(dt, intents)                            // everything in §7, in a fixed order\n             presenter.step(dt)                                 // mirror, rigs, world.step (browser only)","numLines":300,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2135}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EQWkE3tG7pcbYRCjgLWASM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
