{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghfwaa2CHsNtya5YYQvf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the About & Credits pages (Source Information, the three feedback sections, credits). I'll revise the testing details at the end once the test results are in."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2451,"cache_read_input_tokens":756970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2451},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa845e-3b4d-4028-b42c-2fc9910c5b5f","uuid":"3dd8ea07-fb9e-4f26-a49a-0a0f18a91e06","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:44:21.411Z","request_id":"req_011Cghfwa7ywiXfJeTZhgxd8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghfwaa2CHsNtya5YYQvf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DrSfKDqcg5iziBWgYtwecw","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-013140__x696bb3/work/Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-013140/content/about.js","content":"/* About & Credits (§7.20, §16): the hand-back's Source Information and feedback, as written in the chat reply, then the\n * credits. Shown from the title and from the pause menu. */\nS7.ABOUT = {\n  source: [\n    ['Date', '2026-11-04 01:31:40'],\n    ['Source', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)'],\n    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],\n    ['Genre and Perspective', 'On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person scanning and shooting, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. Third-person cinematic rail cameras that hard-cut while moving; first person while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu.'],\n    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the 2D engine with all its modules, patched) and its 3D module v0.85.2; three.js r182 (WebGPU renderer, WebGL 2 fallback) and Rapier 0.19.3 from the jsDelivr CDN; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests.'],\n    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native style: a PS1-resolution 3D picture, characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, ink outlines, and the 3D module\\'s Comic cel filter where the device can draw it (it falls back to the clean picture by itself). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red for blood and focus. New hats, accessories, weapons and skis on the rigs; about sixty new shapes; pen-drawn machinery.'],\n    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural rigs (gaits, poses, moves, expressions) with new hats, accessories, weapons and skis (engine patches); skiing crouch and tuck, quick turns, switch flurries, freezing and thawing, giggle-ups and melting; first-person weapon models with bob, sway, kick and a reload each.'],\n    ['Audio', 'All in code: songs in the engine\\'s step notation with explore and danger layers (a hotel lounge, an oompah polka, a band whose tracks are its weak spots), chip-voice sound effects, a WebAudio laughter synth (four styles, positional, muffled behind walls), Animalese-style gibberish voices typed letter by letter, and wind and room-tone beds.'],\n    ['Online', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode: Echo Run, the Black Run as a 1 to 4 player time trial with the others as echoes. On its own: solo Echo Run against your recorded best run.'],\n    ['Testing', 'Headless Node suites driving game.advance and game.debug (rails, cameras, combat, gates, saves, bosses, attract mode, determinism, a scripted bot playthrough of the whole demo), headless Chromium on the built file with the CDN routed to local copies, and the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players.'],\n    ['Research', 'None beyond the plan, the engine\\'s handoff documents (README, API card, guide, asset card, the 3D module\\'s header) and the AI Hub README.'],\n    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],\n    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],\n    ['Usage', 'unknown']\n  ],\n  testing: [\n    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About forty during the build: the content validator caught missing areas and walls across rails, the Node suites caught softlocks and state bugs, and the browser runs caught black frames and layout overlaps.'],\n    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'About eight: the Comic cel filter drawing a black screen on a GPU-less browser, scenes and boot order, a pause-menu edge case, softlocks in the scripted playthrough, and cameras inside walls.'],\n    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: an honest bot playing the whole demo through the same intents a player makes, which found every softlock.'],\n    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'Pixel screenshots in headless Chromium for look checks: SwiftShader draws without a GPU, so post filters came out black and the pictures said little about a real screen.']\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the 2D engine and its 3D module are one engine: fixed steps, input with rebinding, the pause menu, settings, saves, rigs and the music clock all carried straight into a 3D game, and the rigs drawn as cards gave the characters a look for free.'],\n    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'Yes: the 3D module\\'s post-processing filters render black in headless Chromium without a GPU, so I added a probe that falls back to the clean filter; and the level text format treats a block of one stairs character as a single flight, which I first guessed was per tile.'],\n    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The rail system with its camera director: rails, junctions, ski physics and lifts, and Killer7-style hard-cut shots generated per stretch of rail and checked for line of sight.'],\n    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Working out a 3D game on a 2D-first API surface from documentation spread over several files, and keeping a deterministic sim separate from a presenter that cannot be read back.'],\n    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', 'Every change is marked // S7 PATCH: in my-3d2dge.js. Font glyphs for umlauts, accents, dashes and the PlayStation symbols the prompts need; PlayStation as the default button symbols; in an AI Hub session Options is ignored while Create is held; seven new Humanoid hats; head and body accessories (goggles, moustache, beard, cigar, grin, shades, pack, bandolier, badge, braces, camera); slit eyes; a visor that hides the face; seven new weapons (revolver, pistol, rifle, shotgun, ski poles, alphorn, launcher); skis.'],\n    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'A rail-and-camera primitive for cinematic games (shots along a path with line-of-sight checks), and post filters that degrade gracefully where the GPU path fails.']\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'Its clarity about the finished product: a complete vertical slice with dev tools, attract mode, multiplayer and controller-first UX, plus a detailed plan to build from.'],\n    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan was written for another run\\'s names, model and date, so file names had to follow TASK.md instead; and \"Online\" pointed to a multiplayer library while the source is a single-player game.'],\n    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'Building the whole plan in one go: ten areas, three bosses, seven playable characters, and every system around them.'],\n    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Scope the plan to the time available, or name the features that matter most, and say whether headless screenshots are a reliable judge of the look.']\n  ]\n};\nS7.ABOUT_PAGES = [\n  { title: 'SOURCE INFORMATION', items: S7.ABOUT.source },\n  { title: 'TESTING FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.testing },\n  { title: 'ENGINE FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.engine },\n  { title: 'PROMPT FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.prompt },\n  { title: 'CREDITS', items: [\n    ['', 'SKILLER7 — SEVEN KILLERS, ONE MOUNTAIN. An original game. Design, code, art, music and words by code, from a plan.'],\n    ['Made with', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 and its 3D module (MIT) · three.js r182 (MIT) · Rapier 0.19.3 (Apache-2.0) · AI Hub session client 1.4.0'],\n    ['Asset store entries', 'Converted from Kenney (Holiday, Furniture, Nature) and KayKit (Furniture Bits) CC0 sets.'],\n    ['Inspired by', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005). An original game; no affiliation.']\n  ] }\n];\nS7.CREDITS = [\n  ['', 'SKILLER7', 3], ['', 'SEVEN KILLERS, ONE MOUNTAIN', 1], ['', ''],\n  ['THE SEVEN', 'Ansel · Dagny · Ignaz · Rurik · Kasimir · Mireille · Brigid'], ['IN THE BED', 'Oma Holle'], ['KNITTING', 'Sister Rosalind'], ['ON THE PHONE', 'The Concierge'],\n  ['THE WIND', 'Mister Föhn'], ['COUNTING', 'Pim the Lift Boy'], ['GRATUITY', 'Murmel'], ['CHECKED OUT', 'Augustin Grau · Die Lustigen Vier · Hansi Pulver'], ['', ''],\n  ['ENGINE', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 and its 3D module'], ['3D', 'three.js r182 · Rapier 0.19.3'], ['ONLINE', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1'], ['SHAPES', 'Kenney and KayKit CC0 sets, converted'], ['', ''],\n  ['INSPIRED BY', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)'], ['', 'An original game. No affiliation.'], ['', ''],\n  ['MODEL', 'Anthropic claude-opus-5-5 · xhigh'], ['', 'THE SNOW WILL CONTINUE.', 2]\n];\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2451,"cache_read_input_tokens":756970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2451},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa845e-3b4d-4028-b42c-2fc9910c5b5f","uuid":"341cc9ad-4da2-4979-8007-748453b19362","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:44:53.129Z","request_id":"req_011Cghfwa7ywiXfJeTZhgxd8","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DrSfKDqcg5iziBWgYtwecw":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-013140__x696bb3/work/Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-013140/content/about.js","content":"/* About & Credits (§7.20, §16): the hand-back's Source Information and feedback, as written in the chat reply, then the\n * credits. Shown from the title and from the pause menu. */\nS7.ABOUT = {\n  source: [\n    ['Date', '2026-11-04 01:31:40'],\n    ['Source', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)'],\n    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],\n    ['Genre and Perspective', 'On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person scanning and shooting, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. Third-person cinematic rail cameras that hard-cut while moving; first person while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu.'],\n    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the 2D engine with all its modules, patched) and its 3D module v0.85.2; three.js r182 (WebGPU renderer, WebGL 2 fallback) and Rapier 0.19.3 from the jsDelivr CDN; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests.'],\n    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native style: a PS1-resolution 3D picture, characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, ink outlines, and the 3D module\\'s Comic cel filter where the device can draw it (it falls back to the clean picture by itself). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red for blood and focus. New hats, accessories, weapons and skis on the rigs; about sixty new shapes; pen-drawn machinery.'],\n    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural rigs (gaits, poses, moves, expressions) with new hats, accessories, weapons and skis (engine patches); skiing crouch and tuck, quick turns, switch flurries, freezing and thawing, giggle-ups and melting; first-person weapon models with bob, sway, kick and a reload each.'],\n    ['Audio', 'All in code: songs in the engine\\'s step notation with explore and danger layers (a hotel lounge, an oompah polka, a band whose tracks are its weak spots), chip-voice sound effects, a WebAudio laughter synth (four styles, positional, muffled behind walls), Animalese-style gibberish voices typed letter by letter, and wind and room-tone beds.'],\n    ['Online', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode: Echo Run, the Black Run as a 1 to 4 player time trial with the others as echoes. On its own: solo Echo Run against your recorded best run.'],\n    ['Testing', 'Headless Node suites driving game.advance and game.debug (rails, cameras, combat, gates, saves, bosses, attract mode, determinism, a scripted bot playthrough of the whole demo), headless Chromium on the built file with the CDN routed to local copies, and the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players.'],\n    ['Research', 'None beyond the plan, the engine\\'s handoff documents (README, API card, guide, asset card, the 3D module\\'s header) and the AI Hub README.'],\n    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],\n    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],\n    ['Usage', 'unknown']\n  ],\n  testing: [\n    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About forty during the build: the content validator caught missing areas and walls across rails, the Node suites caught softlocks and state bugs, and the browser runs caught black frames and layout overlaps.'],\n    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'About eight: the Comic cel filter drawing a black screen on a GPU-less browser, scenes and boot order, a pause-menu edge case, softlocks in the scripted playthrough, and cameras inside walls.'],\n    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: an honest bot playing the whole demo through the same intents a player makes, which found every softlock.'],\n    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'Pixel screenshots in headless Chromium for look checks: SwiftShader draws without a GPU, so post filters came out black and the pictures said little about a real screen.']\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the 2D engine and its 3D module are one engine: fixed steps, input with rebinding, the pause menu, settings, saves, rigs and the music clock all carried straight into a 3D game, and the rigs drawn as cards gave the characters a look for free.'],\n    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'Yes: the 3D module\\'s post-processing filters render black in headless Chromium without a GPU, so I added a probe that falls back to the clean filter; and the level text format treats a block of one stairs character as a single flight, which I first guessed was per tile.'],\n    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The rail system with its camera director: rails, junctions, ski physics and lifts, and Killer7-style hard-cut shots generated per stretch of rail and checked for line of sight.'],\n    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Working out a 3D game on a 2D-first API surface from documentation spread over several files, and keeping a deterministic sim separate from a presenter that cannot be read back.'],\n    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', 'Every change is marked // S7 PATCH: in my-3d2dge.js. Font glyphs for umlauts, accents, dashes and the PlayStation symbols the prompts need; PlayStation as the default button symbols; in an AI Hub session Options is ignored while Create is held; seven new Humanoid hats; head and body accessories (goggles, moustache, beard, cigar, grin, shades, pack, bandolier, badge, braces, camera); slit eyes; a visor that hides the face; seven new weapons (revolver, pistol, rifle, shotgun, ski poles, alphorn, launcher); skis.'],\n    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'A rail-and-camera primitive for cinematic games (shots along a path with line-of-sight checks), and post filters that degrade gracefully where the GPU path fails.']\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'Its clarity about the finished product: a complete vertical slice with dev tools, attract mode, multiplayer and controller-first UX, plus a detailed plan to build from.'],\n    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan was written for another run\\'s names, model and date, so file names had to follow TASK.md instead; and \"Online\" pointed to a multiplayer library while the source is a single-player game.'],\n    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'Building the whole plan in one go: ten areas, three bosses, seven playable characters, and every system around them.'],\n    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Scope the plan to the time available, or name the features that matter most, and say whether headless screenshots are a reliable judge of the look.']\n  ]\n};\nS7.ABOUT_PAGES = [\n  { title: 'SOURCE INFORMATION', items: S7.ABOUT.source },\n  { title: 'TESTING FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.testing },\n  { title: 'ENGINE FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.engine },\n  { title: 'PROMPT FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.prompt },\n  { title: 'CREDITS', items: [\n    ['', 'SKILLER7 — SEVEN KILLERS, ONE MOUNTAIN. An original game. Design, code, art, music and words by code, from a plan.'],\n    ['Made with', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 and its 3D module (MIT) · three.js r182 (MIT) · Rapier 0.19.3 (Apache-2.0) · AI Hub session client 1.4.0'],\n    ['Asset store entries', 'Converted from Kenney (Holiday, Furniture, Nature) and KayKit (Furniture Bits) CC0 sets.'],\n    ['Inspired by', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005). An original game; no affiliation.']\n  ] }\n];\nS7.CREDITS = [\n  ['', 'SKILLER7', 3], ['', 'SEVEN KILLERS, ONE MOUNTAIN', 1], ['', ''],\n  ['THE SEVEN', 'Ansel · Dagny · Ignaz · Rurik · Kasimir · Mireille · Brigid'], ['IN THE BED', 'Oma Holle'], ['KNITTING', 'Sister Rosalind'], ['ON THE PHONE', 'The Concierge'],\n  ['THE WIND', 'Mister Föhn'], ['COUNTING', 'Pim the Lift Boy'], ['GRATUITY', 'Murmel'], ['CHECKED OUT', 'Augustin Grau · Die Lustigen Vier · Hansi Pulver'], ['', ''],\n  ['ENGINE', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 and its 3D module'], ['3D', 'three.js r182 · Rapier 0.19.3'], ['ONLINE', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1'], ['SHAPES', 'Kenney and KayKit CC0 sets, converted'], ['', ''],\n  ['INSPIRED BY', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)'], ['', 'An original game. No affiliation.'], ['', ''],\n  ['MODEL', 'Anthropic claude-opus-5-5 · xhigh'], ['', 'THE SNOW WILL CONTINUE.', 2]\n];\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DrSfKDqcg5iziBWgYtwecw","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-013140__x696bb3/work/Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-013140/content/about.js (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afaa845e-3b4d-4028-b42c-2fc9910c5b5f","uuid":"ef7cc01a-b74d-4638-ae7c-09c973877441","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:44:53.140Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-013140__x696bb3/work/Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-013140/content/about.js","content":"/* About & Credits (§7.20, §16): the hand-back's Source Information and feedback, as written in the chat reply, then the\n * credits. Shown from the title and from the pause menu. */\nS7.ABOUT = {\n  source: [\n    ['Date', '2026-11-04 01:31:40'],\n    ['Source', 'Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)'],\n    ['Plan', 'PLAN.md'],\n    ['Genre and Perspective', 'On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person scanning and shooting, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. Third-person cinematic rail cameras that hard-cut while moving; first person while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu.'],\n    ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the 2D engine with all its modules, patched) and its 3D module v0.85.2; three.js r182 (WebGPU renderer, WebGL 2 fallback) and Rapier 0.19.3 from the jsDelivr CDN; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests.'],\n    ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native style: a PS1-resolution 3D picture, characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, ink outlines, and the 3D module\\'s Comic cel filter where the device can draw it (it falls back to the clean picture by itself). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red for blood and focus. New hats, accessories, weapons and skis on the rigs; about sixty new shapes; pen-drawn machinery.'],\n    ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural rigs (gaits, poses, moves, expressions) with new hats, accessories, weapons and skis (engine patches); skiing crouch and tuck, quick turns, switch flurries, freezing and thawing, giggle-ups and melting; first-person weapon models with bob, sway, kick and a reload each.'],\n    ['Audio', 'All in code: songs in the engine\\'s step notation with explore and danger layers (a hotel lounge, an oompah polka, a band whose tracks are its weak spots), chip-voice sound effects, a WebAudio laughter synth (four styles, positional, muffled behind walls), Animalese-style gibberish voices typed letter by letter, and wind and room-tone beds.'],\n    ['Online', 'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode: Echo Run, the Black Run as a 1 to 4 player time trial with the others as echoes. On its own: solo Echo Run against your recorded best run.'],\n    ['Testing', 'Headless Node suites driving game.advance and game.debug (rails, cameras, combat, gates, saves, bosses, attract mode, determinism, a scripted bot playthrough of the whole demo), headless Chromium on the built file with the CDN routed to local copies, and the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players.'],\n    ['Research', 'None beyond the plan, the engine\\'s handoff documents (README, API card, guide, asset card, the 3D module\\'s header) and the AI Hub README.'],\n    ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],\n    ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],\n    ['Usage', 'unknown']\n  ],\n  testing: [\n    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About forty during the build: the content validator caught missing areas and walls across rails, the Node suites caught softlocks and state bugs, and the browser runs caught black frames and layout overlaps.'],\n    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'About eight: the Comic cel filter drawing a black screen on a GPU-less browser, scenes and boot order, a pause-menu edge case, softlocks in the scripted playthrough, and cameras inside walls.'],\n    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: an honest bot playing the whole demo through the same intents a player makes, which found every softlock.'],\n    ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'Pixel screenshots in headless Chromium for look checks: SwiftShader draws without a GPU, so post filters came out black and the pictures said little about a real screen.']\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the 2D engine and its 3D module are one engine: fixed steps, input with rebinding, the pause menu, settings, saves, rigs and the music clock all carried straight into a 3D game, and the rigs drawn as cards gave the characters a look for free.'],\n    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'Yes: the 3D module\\'s post-processing filters render black in headless Chromium without a GPU, so I added a probe that falls back to the clean filter; and the level text format treats a block of one stairs character as a single flight, which I first guessed was per tile.'],\n    ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The rail system with its camera director: rails, junctions, ski physics and lifts, and Killer7-style hard-cut shots generated per stretch of rail and checked for line of sight.'],\n    ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Working out a 3D game on a 2D-first API surface from documentation spread over several files, and keeping a deterministic sim separate from a presenter that cannot be read back.'],\n    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', 'Every change is marked // S7 PATCH: in my-3d2dge.js. Font glyphs for umlauts, accents, dashes and the PlayStation symbols the prompts need; PlayStation as the default button symbols; in an AI Hub session Options is ignored while Create is held; seven new Humanoid hats; head and body accessories (goggles, moustache, beard, cigar, grin, shades, pack, bandolier, badge, braces, camera); slit eyes; a visor that hides the face; seven new weapons (revolver, pistol, rifle, shotgun, ski poles, alphorn, launcher); skis.'],\n    ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'A rail-and-camera primitive for cinematic games (shots along a path with line-of-sight checks), and post filters that degrade gracefully where the GPU path fails.']\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    ['What did you like most about this prompt?', 'Its clarity about the finished product: a complete vertical slice with dev tools, attract mode, multiplayer and controller-first UX, plus a detailed plan to build from.'],\n    ['What was confusing?', 'The plan was written for another run\\'s names, model and date, so file names had to follow TASK.md instead; and \"Online\" pointed to a multiplayer library while the source is a single-player game.'],\n    ['What was the hardest thing to do?', 'Building the whole plan in one go: ten areas, three bosses, seven playable characters, and every system around them.'],\n    ['What would you change in this prompt?', 'Scope the plan to the time available, or name the features that matter most, and say whether headless screenshots are a reliable judge of the look.']\n  ]\n};\nS7.ABOUT_PAGES = [\n  { title: 'SOURCE INFORMATION', items: S7.ABOUT.source },\n  { title: 'TESTING FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.testing },\n  { title: 'ENGINE FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.engine },\n  { title: 'PROMPT FEEDBACK', items: S7.ABOUT.prompt },\n  { title: 'CREDITS', items: [\n    ['', 'SKILLER7 — SEVEN KILLERS, ONE MOUNTAIN. An original game. Design, code, art, music and words by code, from a plan.'],\n    ['Made with', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 and its 3D module (MIT) · three.js r182 (MIT) · Rapier 0.19.3 (Apache-2.0) · AI Hub se